This is a known breaking change. Android deprecated `AdjustResize` (API 30+), and .NET 10 MAUI moved to **edge-to-edge** rendering with the `WindowInsets` API. Here's how to fix it:

### 1. Use `AdjustResize` via the Android manifest (quick fix)

In `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
[Activity(
    Theme = "@style/Maui.SplashTheme",
    MainLauncher = true,
    WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustResize,
    ConfigurationChanges = /* ... */)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity { }
```

> This alone may not work in .NET 10's edge-to-edge mode.

### 2. The .NET 10 approach — handle keyboard insets

In your `MauiProgram.cs`, make sure the new **KeyboardAutoManagerScroll** isn't fighting you, then apply bottom padding reactively:

```csharp
builder
    .UseMauiApp<App>()
    .ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
    {
#if ANDROID
        Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect();
#endif
    });
```

Then in your `MainActivity.cs`, listen for insets and push the content up:

```csharp
using AndroidX.Core.View;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        var rootView = FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!;
        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(rootView, new KeyboardInsetsListener());
    }

    class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
    {
        public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
        {
            var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
            var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());
            int bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
            v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottom);
            return insets;
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. XAML layout (chat screen)

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}"
                    ItemsUpdatingScrollMode="KeepLastItemInView" />

    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" Command="{Binding SendCommand}" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

**Key points:**
- **Approach #2** is the forward-compatible solution — it uses `WindowInsetsCompat` which replaces the deprecated `AdjustResize` behavior.
- `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect()` prevents MAUI's built-in scroll behavior from conflicting with your inset handling.
- The insets listener adds bottom padding equal to the keyboard height, pushing your entire layout up.
- On **iOS**, `AdjustResize`-like behavior still works by default; no extra work needed.
